<% content_for :title, "Paganism & Bipolar | Bipolar Sober" %>

<style>
  .pagan-bipolar-page .cbt-bipolar-hero {
    background-image:
      linear-gradient(
        90deg,
        rgba(8, 8, 8, .9) 0%,
        rgba(8, 8, 8, .58) 45%,
        rgba(8, 8, 8, .12) 100%
      ),
      url("<%= asset_path('bipolar_paganism_desktop.webp') %>");
    background-position: center right;
    background-size: cover;
    background-repeat: no-repeat;
  }

  .pagan-bipolar-page .cbt-copy,
  .pagan-bipolar-page .cbt-copy > div {
    min-width: 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .pagan-bipolar-page .cbt-source {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font-size: 0.75em;
    font-style: normal;
  }

  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .pagan-bipolar-page .cbt-bipolar-hero {
      background-image:
        linear-gradient(
          180deg,
          rgba(8, 8, 8, .28) 0%,
          rgba(8, 8, 8, .55) 48%,
          rgba(8, 8, 8, .92) 100%
        ),
        url("<%= asset_path('bipolar_paganism_mobile.webp') %>");
      background-position: center, center bottom;
    }
  }
</style>

<main class="cbt-bipolar-page pagan-bipolar-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="cbt-bipolar-hero">
    <div class="cbt-bipolar-hero-content">

      <p class="cbt-bipolar-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / BIPOLAR / PERSPECTIVES / PAGANISM
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>BIPOLAR</span>
        <span>THROUGH A</span>
        <span>PAGAN LENS.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="cbt-bipolar-intro">
        THE SEASONS CHANGE.<br>
        SO DO YOU.<br>
        KEEP YOUR ROOTS.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / THE PAGAN PERSPECTIVE -->
  <section class="cbt-section cbt-pink"
           aria-labelledby="pagan-perspective-title">

    <div class="cbt-section-heading">
      <p class="cbt-number">01 / THE PAGAN PERSPECTIVE</p>
      <h2 id="pagan-perspective-title">
        FIND YOUR<br>
        OWN MEANING.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="cbt-copy">

      <blockquote>
        <p>"Any ritual is an opportunity for transformation."</p>
        <cite class="cbt-source">—Starhawk</cite>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Paganism includes many traditions with different beliefs about
        nature, the sacred, ritual, and personal experience. There isn't
        one official Pagan explanation for bipolar disorder.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can draw meaning from nature, spiritual practices, and your
        own experiences without treating your illness as a divine message,
        punishment, or spiritual assignment.
      </p>

      <p>
        Your spirituality can be part of your life without needing to
        explain every part of it.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        YOUR ILLNESS DOESN'T NEED<br>
        A SPIRITUAL EXPLANATION.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / THE CYCLES OF NATURE -->
  <section class="cbt-section cbt-yellow"
           aria-labelledby="pagan-cycles-title">

    <div class="cbt-section-heading">
      <p class="cbt-number">02 / THE CYCLES OF NATURE</p>
      <h2 id="pagan-cycles-title">
        NOTHING<br>
        STAYS THE<br>
        SAME.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="cbt-copy">

      <p>
        Spring brings growth, summer brings abundance, autumn brings
        decline, and winter brings stillness. Nature is always changing.
      </p>

      <p>
        These cycles can offer a metaphor for living with changing
        circumstances, but mania and depression aren't natural forces
        that need to balance each other. They're medical conditions
        that deserve treatment.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can't demand that winter become summer, but you can prepare
        for winter. Likewise, you can't always prevent a bipolar episode,
        but you can recognize patterns, prepare for difficult periods,
        and make choices that protect your stability.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        YOU CAN'T CONTROL EVERY CHANGE.<br>
        YOU CAN PREPARE FOR IT.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / GROWTH AND REST -->
  <section class="cbt-section cbt-black"
           aria-labelledby="pagan-growth-title">

    <div class="cbt-section-heading">
      <p class="cbt-number">03 / GROWTH AND REST</p>
      <h2 id="pagan-growth-title">
        EVEN A<br>
        GARDEN<br>
        NEEDS<br>
        PRUNING.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="cbt-copy">

      <p>
        Your depressive episodes can last four months or longer. That
        isn't a brief seasonal pause or a spiritual lesson you need to
        appreciate. Depression can be exhausting, isolating, and disruptive.
      </p>

      <p>
        During those periods, you can recognize your limitations, accept
        support, and care for yourself without demanding that you function
        normally. Rest and treatment aren't failures of spiritual practice.
      </p>

      <p>
        Mania presents a different challenge. Energy, creativity, spending,
        and new ideas can feel impossible to contain. But a garden doesn't
        benefit from limitless growth. Sometimes it needs tending,
        boundaries, and restraint.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can value your creativity without acting on every impulse.
        Slowing down a decision can protect your money, safety, and
        relationships.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        NOT EVERYTHING THAT GROWS<br>
        NEEDS ROOM TO KEEP GROWING.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / THE BODY AND NATURE -->
  <section class="cbt-section cbt-purple"
           aria-labelledby="pagan-body-title">

    <div class="cbt-section-heading">
      <p class="cbt-number">04 / THE BODY AND NATURE</p>
      <h2 id="pagan-body-title">
        YOU'RE<br>
        PART OF<br>
        NATURE<br>
        TOO.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="cbt-copy">

      <p>
        Many Pagan traditions emphasize a relationship with the natural
        world. That connection includes your own body, with all its
        needs and limitations.
      </p>

      <p>
        Your body needs sleep, food, rest, medical care, and movement
        that respects what you're physically capable of doing. Those
        needs don't disappear when your mind is racing or depression
        makes everything feel pointless.
      </p>

      <p>
        Caring for yourself doesn't have to be complicated or ceremonial.
        Sometimes it means eating something, taking your medication,
        resting, or spending a little time outside.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        CARING FOR YOUR BODY<br>
        IS PART OF CARING FOR YOUR LIFE.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / RITUAL AND SPIRITUALITY -->
  <section class="cbt-section cbt-pink"
           aria-labelledby="pagan-ritual-title">

    <div class="cbt-section-heading">
      <p class="cbt-number">05 / RITUAL AND SPIRITUALITY</p>
      <h2 id="pagan-ritual-title">
        MAKE SPACE<br>
        FOR THE<br>
        SACRED.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="cbt-copy">

      <p>
        Ritual can give you a familiar way to pause, reflect, and connect
        with what matters. Lighting a candle, keeping an altar, making an
        offering, or sitting with meaningful objects can create moments
        of quiet and intention.
      </p>

      <p>
        These practices can support your spiritual life without being
        treatments for bipolar disorder. You can find them meaningful
        while continuing medication and psychiatric care.
      </p>

      <p>
        During mania or psychosis, coincidences may feel unusually
        significant, and thoughts can carry an overwhelming sense of
        certainty. You don't have to abandon your beliefs to question
        an interpretation.
      </p>

      <p>
        A spiritual experience doesn't require you to act immediately,
        spend money, stop treatment, or make a sudden commitment. You can
        pause, talk with someone you trust, and give yourself time before
        deciding what something means.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        SOMETHING CAN FEEL MEANINGFUL<br>
        WITHOUT DEMANDING ACTION.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / PERSONAL AGENCY -->
  <section class="cbt-section cbt-yellow"
           aria-labelledby="pagan-agency-title">

    <div class="cbt-section-heading">
      <p class="cbt-number">06 / PERSONAL AGENCY</p>
      <h2 id="pagan-agency-title">
        YOUR LIFE.<br>
        YOUR<br>
        OBLIGATION.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="cbt-copy">

      <p>
        Pagan spirituality can leave room to explore traditions, choose
        personal rituals, and decide what holds meaning for you. That
        freedom doesn't mean you can control every part of bipolar disorder.
      </p>

      <p>
        You didn't choose your diagnosis, and spiritual practice alone
        can't make symptoms disappear. But you can choose how you respond,
        seek support, set boundaries, and care for yourself.
      </p>

      <p>
        Your illness can help explain why certain decisions became
        difficult to manage. It doesn't erase their consequences.
        You can recognize the role of an episode while still acknowledging
        what happened and repairing what you can.
      </p>

      <p>
        You don't have to turn every period of suffering into a lesson.
        Sometimes the meaningful thing is simply continuing to take care
        of yourself and staying connected to the life you want to maintain.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        YOU AREN'T RESPONSIBLE FOR HAVING BIPOLAR DISORDER.<br>
        YOU ARE RESPONSIBLE FOR HOW YOU CARE FOR YOURSELF.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- CLOSING -->
  <section class="cbt-ending"
           aria-labelledby="pagan-closing-title">

    <div>

      <p class="cbt-ending-small">
        CLOSING
      </p>

      <h2 id="pagan-closing-title">
        STAY<br>
        <span>ROOTED.</span>
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="cbt-ending-copy">

      <p>
        You don't have to call bipolar disorder a blessing, a curse,
        or a spiritual assignment. Your beliefs, rituals, and connection
        to nature can remain meaningful through depression, mania, and
        everything in between.
      </p>

      <p>
        The world keeps changing. You can keep caring for yourself
        through those changes.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO PERSPECTIVES",
          bipolar_perspectives_path,
          class: "cbt-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>